    "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n               <jats:p>We document that persistent aggregate capital flows to hedge and mutual funds predict monthly factor returns with an out-of-sample R2 reaching 6.6%. Transient flows display no such power despite being more predictable. We show\u2014both empirically and theoretically\u2014that persistent flows\u2019 predictive power stems from active fund managers\u2019 capital constraints. As a result, managers invest persistent, but not transient, capital flows into factor trading strategies, leading to factor-return predictability and factor momentum, yet greater price efficiency. Our key insight is that capital-constrained managers account for both current and anticipated future flows in the arbitrage sector, thereby incorporating the dynamics of capital into their strategies.</jats:p>",
